        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: differō, differre",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "differō, differre — \"to put off; to differ; to spread\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Rem in crāstinum diem distulimus. We put the matter off to the next day."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "differō, differre, distulī, dīlātum: irregular, like ferō. To put off, and, without an object, to differ. English defer and differ."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "differō is built from dis- \"apart\" and ferō \"to carry\" (EtymDB; Lewis & Short): to carry in different directions, or forward in time. English defer and differ both come from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
